import{_ as s,o as t,c as n,a2 as o}from"./chunks/framework.7R1OEGYz.js";const c=JSON.parse('{"title":"The Workspace view","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"workspace.md","filePath":"workspace.md","lastUpdated":1794162284000}'),a={name:"workspace.md"};function r(i,e,h,d,l,g){return t(),n("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<h1 id="the-workspace-view" tabindex="-1">The Workspace view <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-workspace-view" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The Workspace view&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The Workspace shows an interactive session&#39;s conversation as a chat: what you asked, what the agent answered, and a compact log of the tools it used. Next to it you can open the session&#39;s files, the Changes view and the integrated browser, and a terminal drawer when you need the agent&#39;s own console.</p><p><strong>Edition:</strong> Community · <strong>Who:</strong> the session owner. A Collaborator on a <a href="/docs/sharing">shared session</a> (Enterprise) can read and write messages too; a Viewer can only read.</p><h2 id="workspace-or-terminal" tabindex="-1">Workspace or Terminal <a class="header-anchor" href="#workspace-or-terminal" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Workspace or Terminal&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each session opens in one of two interfaces, chosen under <strong>Interface</strong> when you create it:</p><ul><li><strong>Workspace</strong>: conversation, tasks and tools in one view.</li><li><strong>Terminal</strong>: the agent CLI&#39;s own console interface.</li></ul><p>The default for new sessions is set per browser under <strong>Settings → Interface</strong>. You can switch a running session between the two with the <strong>Workspace</strong> and <strong>Terminal</strong> buttons in its session bar, without restarting the agent.</p><h2 id="the-session-bar" tabindex="-1">The session bar <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-session-bar" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The session bar&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The bar above the conversation stays on one line on desktop and tablet widths; on a phone it uses two (title and <strong>⋯</strong> first, the controls below). It always shows:</p><ul><li>the session&#39;s title and status,</li><li>the <strong>Model</strong> chip (see <a href="/docs/models">Models</a>),</li><li>a small dot for the connection to the agent; hover it, or read it with a screen reader, for the words (<em>connected</em>, <em>connecting…</em>, <em>disconnected</em>),</li><li>the <strong>Workspace / Terminal / Shell</strong> switch,</li><li><strong>Pause</strong> or <strong>Resume</strong>, and <strong>Share</strong> (owner).</li></ul><p>Everything else is in the <strong>⋯</strong> menu at the end of the bar:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Entry</th><th>Who sees it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Account</strong> — the login the session runs on; see <a href="/docs/provider-login#several-accounts-per-provider">Provider logins</a></td><td>Owner, subscription sessions with several logins</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Switch provider…</strong> — see <a href="/docs/provider-switching">Switch provider</a></td><td>Owner</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Files</strong>, and <strong>Browser</strong> while the agent&#39;s browser runs</td><td>Everyone who can open the session</td></tr><tr><td><strong>✉ Messages</strong></td><td>Owner</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Edit session</strong>, <strong>Continue locally</strong> (Claude)</td><td>Owner</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Update now</strong>, when the session runs an outdated runtime image</td><td>Owner</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A dot on the <strong>⋯</strong> button means something inside wants attention: open messages, an outdated image, or a provider switch in progress. On narrow windows <strong>Pause</strong> and <strong>Share</strong> show only their icon; their names stay available as tooltips and to screen readers. With the keyboard, open the menu with Enter, move with Tab or the arrow keys, and close it with Esc.</p><p>A Viewer, and anyone opening a share link, sees the view switch, a <strong>Read-only</strong> mark and a menu with <strong>Files</strong> (and <strong>Browser</strong>) only.</p><p>In the Workspace, <strong>⌘ Agent terminal</strong> opens the agent&#39;s console in a drawer below the conversation, and <strong>›_ Shell</strong> opens a separate shell in the session&#39;s container. The shell is only offered to the session owner.</p><h2 id="the-conversation" tabindex="-1">The conversation <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-conversation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The conversation&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>New messages appear live while the agent works. Long conversations open with the most recent part; <strong>Load earlier messages</strong> goes further back.</li><li><strong>Find in conversation…</strong> searches the messages that are loaded.</li><li>Each message has <strong>Copy</strong> and <strong>Quote</strong> actions; <strong>Quote</strong> puts the text into your draft.</li><li>Hover a time stamp for the full date and time.</li><li>The conversation survives a pause, a restart and a runtime update, and it follows the agent&#39;s <code>/clear</code> and <code>/new</code>.</li><li>Long links and inline code wrap; code blocks and tables scroll on their own.</li></ul><h2 id="opencode-conversations" tabindex="-1">OpenCode conversations <a class="header-anchor" href="#opencode-conversations" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;OpenCode conversations&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Choose <strong>OpenCode</strong> and <strong>Workspace</strong> when creating a session, or switch an existing OpenCode session to Workspace from its header. The conversation shows your messages, completed answers, tool calls and tool results from OpenCode&#39;s own history. Open <strong>Agent terminal</strong> for the CLI&#39;s menus, login and an answer while it is still streaming.</p><p>Earlier messages remain available after reconnecting; use <strong>Load earlier messages</strong> in a long conversation. History catches up in bounded pages. A message appears after OpenCode has saved it; answers appear when complete. Long text and tool output may be shortened, with an omission notice. Native subagent conversations and reasoning are not shown as separate conversations.</p><p>This is included in <strong>Community Edition</strong> for session owners. With <strong>Enterprise</strong> sharing, Viewers can read the conversation and Collaborators can also send messages, subject to their existing session permissions. Shared reads use the same masking as other session history. OpenCode&#39;s managed tool policy and approval prompts still apply to tools started from the CLI.</p><h2 id="the-composer" tabindex="-1">The composer <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-composer" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The composer&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Type in <strong>Give your agent a task…</strong> and press <strong>Enter</strong> to send (<strong>Shift+Enter</strong> for a new line).</p><ul><li><strong>Slash commands:</strong> type <code>/</code> to list the commands of the session&#39;s agent CLI, including custom commands and, where the CLI offers them, skills. Up and Down move the highlight, Tab or Enter complete it, Escape closes the list.</li><li><strong>Suggestions:</strong> when the CLI predicts your next message, it appears greyed out in the input. <strong>→</strong> or <strong>Tab</strong> accepts it into your draft; you still send it yourself. Typing dismisses it.</li><li><strong>Attachments:</strong> paste an image, drop files onto the composer, or use <strong>＋ Attach files</strong>. The files are uploaded to the session&#39;s <a href="/docs/files">files</a> and the agent receives them with your message; images show as thumbnails in the conversation.</li><li><strong>◼ Stop</strong> interrupts the agent&#39;s current turn.</li></ul><p>Your draft is kept per session while you look at another one.</p><h2 id="messages-that-wait" tabindex="-1">Messages that wait <a class="header-anchor" href="#messages-that-wait" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Messages that wait&quot;">​</a></h2><p>When the agent is busy, a message you send is shown as pending until the agent takes it:</p><ul><li><strong>Send now</strong> delivers it into the running turn as a priority message instead of waiting for the turn to end.</li><li><strong>Interrupt and send</strong> stops the agent&#39;s turn, and the agent then takes your message at once.</li><li><strong>✕</strong> stops showing it as pending.</li></ul><p>While the agent is not ready yet (for example while the session is starting), messages go into a queue above the composer, marked <em>Queued · will be sent when the agent is ready</em>. <strong>Edit</strong> puts one back into the composer, <strong>Withdraw</strong> removes it.</p><h2 id="messages-from-other-sessions" tabindex="-1">Messages from other sessions <a class="header-anchor" href="#messages-from-other-sessions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Messages from other sessions&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>⋯ → ✉ Messages</strong> in the session bar (owner only) opens a panel with the messages other sessions sent to this one. While messages are open, the <strong>⋯</strong> button carries a dot and the menu entry a count. <strong>Message this agent</strong> sends your own message; tick <strong>priority</strong> to deliver it into the running prompt now, and <strong>interrupt</strong> to stop the current work first. How sessions message each other is described in <a href="/docs/session-tools">Built-in tools inside a session</a>.</p><h2 id="files-changes-and-the-browser" tabindex="-1">Files, Changes and the browser <a class="header-anchor" href="#files-changes-and-the-browser" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Files, Changes and the browser&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The session bar&#39;s <strong>⋯</strong> menu opens companion panes beside the conversation:</p><ul><li><strong>Files</strong>: the session&#39;s files, with previews. See <a href="/docs/files">Session files</a>.</li><li><strong>Changes</strong>, a view inside the Files pane: a per-file Git diff of the agent&#39;s work in the repositories under the workspace. It needs a running session.</li><li><strong>Browser</strong>: the integrated browser, once the agent has started it. See <a href="/docs/browser">Integrated browser</a>.</li></ul><p>Drag the separator between the conversation and the pane to resize it between 25 % and 75 %; with the keyboard, focus the separator and use the arrow keys. On a phone the panes take turns with the conversation.</p><h2 id="starting-waiting-and-stopped-states" tabindex="-1">Starting, waiting and stopped states <a class="header-anchor" href="#starting-waiting-and-stopped-states" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Starting, waiting and stopped states&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Session starting</strong> shows the stage: waiting for the pod, then starting the agent. It ends when the agent can take input.</li><li>An activity line shows whether the agent is working or waiting for you.</li><li>When the session is paused, finished, failed or scheduled, the composer is replaced by a bar that says so. The owner gets <strong>Resume</strong> there. A finished autonomous run offers to <a href="/docs/sessions#continue-an-autonomous-run-interactively">continue interactively</a>.</li><li>If a CLI needs a sign-in, a sign-in card appears in the conversation; see <a href="/docs/provider-login">Provider login</a>.</li></ul><p>Questions and permission requests from the agent appear in the Workspace as well; see <a href="/docs/questions-approvals">Questions and approvals</a>.</p><h2 id="limits" tabindex="-1">Limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Limits&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>The message-by-message conversation is available for Claude, Codex and OpenCode, which keep a native conversation record. For Cursor and OpenClaw the Workspace shows the cleaned terminal history instead, without per-message times; use the Terminal for those agents when you need the full console.</li></ul><h2 id="preparing-a-provider-handover" tabindex="-1">Preparing a provider handover <a class="header-anchor" href="#preparing-a-provider-handover" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Preparing a provider handover&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The upcoming <a href="/docs/handover-preview">handover preview API</a> lets an owner review a bounded text selection. It does not yet switch the running provider or send the text to another agent.</p>',42)])])}const u=s(a,[["render",r]]);export{c as __pageData,u as default};
